iption rather than a disease name. I want to verify this against other true positives like \"Coats disease\" to see if they all share the canonical \"[Modifier] + disease-noun\" compound structure that the prepositional phrasing lacks.\n\nI'm noticing both parenthetical examples place \"retinal telangiectasis\" as a clarifying gloss rather than a primary mention, while the other examples embed the disease term as a complement of a predicate like \"characterized by,\" \"responsible for,\" or \"presenting as.\" This suggests the pattern isn't about the head noun itself but about the disease mention being structurally embedded—appearing as an argument within a defining or descriptive clause rather than as a standalone noun phrase.\n\nThis suggests \"Coats telangiectasis\" works because the eponym anchors it as a disease name, so the real feature distinguishing TP from FN is whether an identity-anchoring element (eponym, \"disease\" head, acronym) is present versus a bare descriptive phenotype phrase. The discrepancies I'm seeing all lack that anchor—purely descriptive modifiers like \"unilateral,\" \"retinal,\" or biochemical phrasing like \"deficient activity of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase\" without a naming cue, which the model reads as symptoms rather than disease identities.\n\nI'm also noticing a lexical marker: \"so-called\" signals nomenclature (this is the name of a disease), while \"characterized by\" signals symptom description instead. But the G6PD deficiency example complicates things since \"deficiency\" alone can function as a condition name, so the head noun isn't fully decisive on its own—I need to pin down the real boolean distinction between manifestation-level phrasing and true condition nomenclature.\n\nTesting this against all five discrepancies: unilateral retinal telangiectasis and retinal telangiectasis both lack any eponym or disease-type head, deficient activity of FALDH reads as a state description, and deficiency in G6PD similarly lacks a n