rrectly because the eponym anchors it.\n  - \"deficient activity of FALDH\" is a PP-complement enzyme-state description. \"mental retardation,\" a stock term in the same list, is tagged correctly, so list position is not the cause.\n  - \"presenting as vasculitis\" is a bare, unanchored term in a manifestation frame. It fits the anchor hypothesis, but it is a lexicalized term, so it is the weakest fit. The gold label (SpecificDisease for a generic class term) may also be inconsistent.\n\nOverall, the single feature that separates the cases is whether the span is a **fixed nomenclature unit** or an **ad hoc descriptive phrase**. Four of the five cases fit cleanly.\n\n**Step 2: Formulate Pattern**\n\n- **Pattern Insight: Ad hoc phenotype descriptions mistaken for clinical findings (missing nomenclature anchor)**\n  - **Pattern Name:** Unanchored Disease Descriptor Misread as Finding.\n  - **Confusion Trigger:** A condition-denoting head that is a sign, process, or enzyme-state noun (*telangiectasis*, *deficiency*, *deficient activity*, *vasculitis*), realized as a compositional modifier stack or a prepositional complement (\"deficiency **in** G6PD,\" \"deficient activity **of** X\"), or as a bare term following a manifestation frame (\"presenting as,\" \"characterized by\"). The model treats these as descriptions of a phenotype and assigns O.\n  - **Contrastive Evidence:** The True Positives are anchored by an eponym (\"Coats telangiectasis\"), an acronym (\"SLS\"), a premodifier compound (\"G6PD deficiency,\" the same head and gene token as the missed \"deficiency in G6PD\"), or a stock term (\"mental retardation,\" tagged correctly even in a feature list where a paraphrased descriptor was missed).\n  - **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Label a span as SpecificDisease if its head denotes a condition and the span functions as a nomenclature unit: (a) eponym or acronym plus head, (b) gene/enzyme-premodified compound (\"G6PD deficiency\"), or (c) a stock term. Also label 